      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2012. Published by Elsevier Inc.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "Body weight loss is a well-known complication after gastrectomy, and is mainly due to reduced fat volume. The effect of vagotomy on the postoperative fat volume was investigated in patients with early stage gastric cancer who underwent gastrectomy."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "Subcutaneous fat area (SFA) and visceral fat area (VFA) were separately measured in a computed tomographic (CT) image at the level of the umbilicus using Fat Scan software. The changes in these two fat areas were determined by comparing CT images taken before and more than 6 mo after gastrectomy, and the ratio of postoperative to preoperative fat area was calculated in 77 patients."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "VFA was reduced significantly greater after total gastrectomy (TG) than distal gastrectomy (DG) (P = 0.0003). In 63 patients who underwent DG, the reduction in VFA, but not in SFA, was significantly less in vagus nerve-preserved than in vagus nerve-nonpreserved cases (59.0% ± 24.2% versus 74.9% ± 28.2%, P = 0.027). If compared in each case, VFA showed a significantly greater decrease than did SFA in vagus-nonpreserving, but not in vagus-preserving, gastrectomy (68.2% ± 37.0% versus 52.7% ± 25.2%, P < 0.0001; 76.3% ± 30.0% versus 74.9% ± 28.2%, P = 0.79)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "The vagus nerve has a function to locally regulate the amount of intra-abdominal fat tissue, and selective vagotomy in gastrectomy results in a preferential reduction of visceral fat in gastrectomy. Surgical denervation of vagus may be reconsidered as a reasonable treatment for excessive obesity."
          }
        ]
      },
